   "content": "Conclusion: no evacuation is needed now. The 3.1 GB Swap Used and 1.2 GB Compressed are historic reclamation, and with ESXi Memory State High and Host Balloon 0 all day there is no current memory pressure on esx-21.\n\nMechanism and reason\nESXi Memory State High means the host never entered Soft/Hard during the past day, so no reclaim is happening now. Ballooning starts at Soft, compression/host swapping at Hard. Host balloon is the sum of the VMs’ ballooned memory. Balloon 0 all day with State High is consistent with no pressure.\n\nBallooned, Compressed and Swapped are past-tense. On ESXi they are what was reclaimed earlier, not current pressure; one can be non-zero while another is zero because reclaim order depends on state and per-VM settings. Swapped is what sits in the swap file now; Swap In/Out rates and Compression/Decompression rates are 0, so nothing is moving. VMs wait only on swap-in/decompression, which shows in VM Memory Latency, not in ESXi Memory Latency. ESXi Memory Latency is a normalized average of VM latencies and hides the VM that suffers; use the maximum VM Memory Contention across the host’s VMs.\n\nESXi Memory Usage maps to ESXi Memory Consumed. Usage % is Consumed/Total Capacity and is a capacity reporting metric; memory is a cache so near-100% is expected. Pressure shows in ballooning, compression, swapping and VM Memory Contention, not in Usage %.\n\nWhat to check next\nPer-VM VM Memory Latency / Memory Contention, swap-in rate and decompression rate for the worst VM on the host; per-VM Balloon Target vs Ballooned and any VM or resource-pool memory limit that could have caused historic swap/compression; confirm host Consumed vs Total Capacity for capacity context.",
